BLM:-

- The 2020 murder of George Floyd in police custody led to global outrage
- Led to several large protests in the Summer of 2020 in the UK
- Edward Colston statue toppled in Bristol - donated around Bristol heavily but his wealth came from slave trading
- Taking the knee at football events - Euro 2020 England team took the knee
- Led to large discussions - Johnson government established a commission on racial inequality.
- Several companies and organisations released statements condemning racism
- Bristol's Colston Hall became the Bristol Beacon an Colston's Girls' School became Montpellier high school
- From 2022 the Welsh government announced teaching the history of BAME people would be mandatory
- March 2020 → March 2021 racially motivated crime increased by 12% to its highest ever level
- Priti Patel - Home secretary condemned taking the Knee and denounced people who toppled statues as vandals
- It has raised awareness but people contend if it has caused real change

Extinction Rebellion:-

- Grass roots social movement opposed to traditional pressure groups like Greenpeace
- Its members favour 'non-violent civil obedience' designed to challenge public and political complacency on climate change.
- It wants the government to commit to net zero by 2025 and declare a climate emergency and uses direct action to do this
- It encourages people to join protests and calls for 'peoples assemblies' which are a form of direct democracy
- Dug up the lawn at Trinity College, Cambridge to draw attention to sell land for a lorry park to be built on
- In 2021, they blocked Amazon distribution centres to highlight their negative environmental impact
- Disrupted the Lord mayors show in London by blocking traffic in Covent Garden to protest the state of affairs at COP 26
- 'They are loud, they are disruptive, they sometimes throw paint and other such things, and they block, but what else is going to make politicians really recalibrate, realign, revolutionise politics so that it faces the horrors of climate change and all the damage that we are doing to our planet?' - Stephen Fry
- Others are more critical and say they alienate the public
- Professor of Geography at Cambridge University Mike Hulme says that 'the forms of protests that Extinction Rebellion employs … risk alienating the large majority of the public upon which any sensible climate policies must rely.'

